NodeJS LabsIn this labs session, I would request you as a reader to stay focussed and complete all the experiments in one seating. Practice these experiments in your free time. Memorise it if needed. But commit to it. I shall keep adding more experiments
Experiment 1: The Cryptographic Chef-Off
This experiment proves that the Thread Pool (the extra back chefs) handles heavy calculations. By default, libuv has 4 threads. We will order 4 complex dishes at once, and then 5 complex dishes, to see the bottleneck.
Setup the Code
Create a file named threadpool-test.js and paste this code. It uses pbkdf2 (a password hashing function) which represents a heavy, CPU-intensive kitchen task.
const crypto = require('crypto');
const start = Date.now();function cookComplexDish(orderNumber) {
// Heavy password hashing representing a blocking task
crypto.pbkdf2('secret', 'salt', 100000, 64, 'sha512', () => {
console.log(`🍳 Order #${orderNumber} finished in: ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
});
}
// Order 4 complex dishes simultaneously
cookComplexDish(1);
cookComplexDish(2);
cookComplexDish(3);
cookComplexDish(4);
Run with 4 Orders
Execute the file in your terminal:
node threadpool-test.jsWhat you will see: All four orders finish at almost the exact same time (e.g., around 300ms), because libuv hands them to its 4 default back chefs simultaneously.
Run with 5 Orders
Add a fifth call to the bottom of your file (cookComplexDish(5);) and run it again.
What you will see: Orders 1 through 4 finish around 300ms. Order 5 takes double the time (around 600ms)!
But Why? The 4 back chefs were busy. Order 5 had to wait in line until one chef finished and became free.
Hire More Chefs (Change Thread Pool Size)
You can dynamically increase the Thread Pool size using an environment variable before running the script. Try running this command in your terminal:

• Linux/macOS: UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=5 node threadpool-test.js

• Windows (PowerShell): $env:UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=5; node threadpool-test.js
What you will see now: All 5 orders finish at the exact same time again! You successfully hired a 5th back chef.

Experiment 2: The Oven vs. The Back Chefs
This experiment proves that Network requests (using the OS kernel “oven”) do not block the Thread Pool chefs.
Setup the Code
Create a file named oven-vs-chefs.js:
const crypto = require('crypto');
const https = require('https');
const start = Date.now();// 1. The Oven Task (Network Request)
function orderFromSupplier() {
https.request('https://google.com', (res) => {
res.on('data', () => {});
res.on('end', () => {
console.log(`🌐 Network Order finished in: ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
});
}).end();
}
// 2. The Back Chef Task (CPU bound)
function cookComplexDish(orderNumber) {
crypto.pbkdf2('secret', 'salt', 100000, 64, 'sha512', () => {
console.log(`🍳 Chef Pool Order #${orderNumber} finished in: ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
});
}
// We trigger 1 network request and 4 heavy chef tasks
orderFromSupplier();
cookComplexDish(1);
cookComplexDish(2);
cookComplexDish(3);
cookComplexDish(4);
Run the Experiment
node oven-vs-chefs.jsAnalyze the Results
If the network request needed a Thread Pool chef, one of the cookComplexDish tasks would be delayed (making it 5 tasks competing for 4 slots). Instead, you will see that the network request finishes independently, and all 4 heavy CPU tasks still finish together!
Why? Libuv registers the network request directly with your Operating System's network stack (like epoll on Linux or kqueue on macOS). It acts like a smart kitchen timer—zero chef muscle required until the timer rings.
